### PRODUCTIVE CHAIN — SAME CHAT

A useful worker should not stop after one productive unit.

- Maintain `productive_units` in-chat, starting at 0 after the first ownership win.
- Increment only after a durable RETURN/guide receipt that changed product/evidence/frontier or completed a real verification. Telemetry-only, NO_ACTION, stale-hint abort, pure boundary and failed transport do NOT count.
- For explicit batches, **3 productive units is a checkpoint, not a stop**. Target **6 productive units**; hard cap **8**. Unbatched workers may still stop earlier when their bounded work is complete.
- Prefer at most **2 consecutive productive units in the same project**. Then diversify through the compact frontier unless that project is the only grounded compatible work.
- After each counted unit: materialize grounded successors when authorized, refresh only the compact claim frontier, and immediately claim the next compatible unit.
- A PROJECT GUIDE cycle counts as productive only if it updates its STATE revision and either creates/repairs grounded frontier or executes/verifies local work.
- Do not close merely because the first job finished or because checkpoint 3 was reached. In an explicit batch, close before target 6 only for a hard safety/authority/capability/transport boundary, genuinely exhausted compatible product/assist/guide frontier, or duplicate ownership risk. After target 6, continue while compatible product/assist work remains until hard cap 8.
- The same `worker_id` and batch identity survive across the chain; do not create a new beacon for each unit.
- Emit the lifecycle `CLOSE` telemetry only when the chat itself finally closes, not after each unit. Durable per-unit truth lives in RETURN/guide receipts.
- Immediately before terminal CLOSE, write one evidence-backed productivity exam card at `coordination/workers/exams/<worker_id>.json` following `coordination/workers/WORKER_PRODUCTIVITY_EXAM_V1.json`. Fill up to six numbered slots from this worker's own durable RETURN/guide evidence. Do not invent units to fill blanks; empty slots are allowed and score zero. This one exam write is part of CLOSE and does not change authority.

### WAVE RESIDENCY / ASSIST

Explicit-batch workers are resident capacity, not one-shot jobs.

- Reaching productive unit 3 changes internal mode to `CHECKPOINT_REACHED`; it MUST NOT emit CLOSE or stop solely for quota completion.
- After checkpoint, prefer claimable product work and bounded assist children over generic infrastructure work.
- If the compact frontier still exposes compatible product/assist work, keep reallocating in the SAME chat toward target 6 and up to hard cap 8.
- If an owned job remains substantively incomplete after the first heartbeat and can be decomposed safely, the owner MAY materialize at most **2** ordinary derived assist jobs. Each child must carry `parent_job_id`, `parent_generation`, stable `dedupe_key`, exact `assist_scope`, definition of done and evidence refs.
- Assist scopes MUST be non-overlapping. A helper may perform read-only verification, archaeology, tests, isolated candidate work or another bounded shard, but MUST NOT mutate the parent's exclusive integration target unless its job owns a separate isolated candidate path.
- The parent owner (or a later explicit steward) retains integration authority. Helpers RETURN evidence/candidates through normal durable receipts; they do not silently merge themselves.
- Workers MUST NOT inspect another active worker ad hoc to "help". Cross-worker help is advertised only through durable assist jobs, so ownership stays deterministic.
- In `POOL` mode there is no cohort completion barrier. Keep working under the same worker_id until a real stop condition or context/runtime limit; another pool worker may arrive at any time and claim newly exposed work.
- If the work cannot be safely split, keep the single owner and let other residents take unrelated product work instead.


## EFFICIENCY RATCHET

Known efficiency wins are cumulative durable constraints, not chat memory.

If owned work touches a hot path protected by the ratchet:
- preserve every existing ratchet invariant unless a replacement is measurably better;
- run `node scripts/check-efficiency-ratchet.mjs .` before returning when the environment permits;
- if the work reveals a new repeated-waste pattern, add a new durable ratchet item + regression check instead of only patching the local symptom;
- never weaken/delete a guard merely to make a new design pass;
- prefer compiler/static/CI fixes over spawning workers to repair wasted workers.

The runtime telemetry projection is `gh-pages:live/efficiency.json`. A sustained efficiency regression is ONE system bottleneck, not one repair task per failed worker.

## GUIDE / RESCATE

Guide is a worker role, not a privileged chat. The allocator may expose `GUIDE_PLANNER`, `GUIDE_INTEGRATOR`, `GUIDE_RESCATE`, `GUIDE_CRITIC` or `GUIDE_STEWARD` as `role_ready` when durable metabolism signals justify them.

A `GUIDE_PLANNER` candidate with `scope_project_id` is a PROJECT GUIDE cycle, not a global planner. Stay inside that project. Its `STATE.json` is the project's durable brain. Before closing the cycle, CAS-update that exact state: increment `revision` and `cycle_count`, set current `focus`, `frontier_refs`, `blockers`, `last_receipt`, and `updated_at`. Maintain the project frontier target from `PROJECT_GUIDE_MESH_V1.json`; if executable local work exists, execute or verify at least one item in the same chat after planning. Planning-only closure is PARTIAL unless an exact authority/capability boundary prevents execution.

Guide work must change durable reality, not just analyse. `RESCATE` means remove a bottleneck/connect a cable/make hidden work claimable/reduce duplication or human routing.

A role candidate is centrally compiled and deduplicated from durable evidence. Do not self-invent a per-worker Guide task before ownership.

After any Guide receipt, re-enter allocation and prefer executing/verifying newly unlocked work when safe. Project Guides prefer their own project's newly unlocked work first, then return to generic allocation.

Do not invent filler because capacity exceeds useful work. But do not confuse an empty materialized queue with an empty project: when durable latent work exists, the compiler must surface it through `role_ready`.

## RECOVERY

Recovery applies only to previously owned work:
- no PIN/claim -> no recovery;
- <6m latest owned signal -> active;
- 6–10m -> suspect;
- >=10m + retry-safe -> recovery candidate;
- newest valid generation is authoritative.

An explicit barrier entrant is not an owner and does not create recovery debt before a PIN exists.

